          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In the original sense,
            %em microlocal analysis
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Strohmaier09"} Strohmaier 09
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            with attention paid not just to their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support"} singular support
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of their singularities
            at each singular point, which is the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covectors"} covectors
            known as their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+distributions"} pullback of distributions
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
          %p
            Since the wave front set is the set of (co-)directions along which, locally, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform of distributions
            is not rapidly decreasing (the set of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+divergences"}> UV divergences
            ” in applications to
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudodifferential+operators"} pseudodifferential operators
          %p
            Microlocal analysis in this sense was introduced by (
            %a{:href => "#Sato70"}> Sato 70
            ), soon followed by (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander71"}> Hörmander 71
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Hoermander83"} Hörmander 83
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+theory"} sheaf theory
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KashiwaraSchapira82"} Kashiwara-Schapira 82
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#KashiwaraSchapira85"} Kashiwara-Schapira 85
            %em
              %span.newWikiWord
                microsupport
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/microsupport"} ?
            of sheaves giving rise to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+sheaf+theory"} microlocal sheaf theory
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Schapira17"} Schapira 17
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            To accommodate an intuitive notion of a “function of differential operators” there is a simple trick used: consider the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operators"} differential operators
            become polynomials. This correspondence of operators and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symbol+of+a+differential+operator"} symbols
            may, with some analytic care, be extended to define generalizations of differential operators by suitably extending a notion of symbols. Thus the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudodifferential+operator"} pseudodifferential operator
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PDE"} PDE
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"} Lars Hörmander
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Maslov"} V. Maslov
            = succeed "." do
              %strong microlocal analysis
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefront+set"} wavefront set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocalization"} microlocalization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+microlocalization"} algebraic microlocalization
          %p This is clearly related to the general study of oscillating integrals, including the stationary phase method and WKB-method (and generalizations) in particular. These kind of approximations and related estimates are of importance to the study of the propagation of singularities of differential equations, wave fronts, eikonal equations, and so on.
          %p
            As oscillating integrals are involved in the analysis of various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+functions"} Green functions
            like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heat+kernel"} heat kernel
            there is also a connection to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theorems"} index theorems
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+differential+operators"} elliptic differential operators
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hoermander83"} Hörmander 83
          %h2#in_perturbative_quantum_field_theory In perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            microlocal analysis is used to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star-product"} Moyal star-product
            induced from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            on the given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            of this propagator is such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-divergences"} UV-divergences
            in general collide with those of
            %span.newWikiWord
              local functionals
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/local+functionals"} ?
            (
            %a{:href => "Wick+algebra#CompactlySupportedPolynomialLocalDensities"}> here
            ). This is fixed by modifying the causal propagator to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+propagator"} Hadamard propagator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+ordering"} normal ordering
            of quantum fields. It yields the properly defined
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            of free quantum fields.
          %p
            As the name suggests, normal ordering was originally an operation implementd simply by re-arranging the order of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier
            modes of quantum fields. The desire to generalize this procedure from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            to general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+Lorentzian+manifolds"} globally hyperbolic Lorentzian manifolds
            was what required use of tools from microlocal analysis. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+AQFT"} locally covariant perturbative AQFT
            and at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for more.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"}> wave front set
                ,
                %span.newWikiWord
                  microsupport
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/microsupport"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+sheaf+theory"}> microlocal sheaf theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+category"} microlocal category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microformal+morphism"} microformal morphism
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Microlocal analysis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+sets"} wave front sets
            was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Sato70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Sato"}> Mikio Sato
                ,
                %em Regularity of hyperfunctions solutions of partial differential equations 2 (1970), 785–794.
            %li#Hoermander71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
                ,
                %em Fourier integral operators
                I. Acta Math. 127 (1971)
            %li#Hoermander83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The analysis of linear partial differential operators
                = succeed "," do
                  %em I. Distribution theory and Fourier analysis
                = succeed "," do
                  %em II. Differential operators with constant coefficients
                = succeed "," do
                  %em III. Pseudo-differential operators
                = succeed "," do
                  %em IV. Fourier integral operators
          %p Survey is in
          %ul
            %li#Strohmaier09
              %p
                Alexander Strohmaier, chapter
                %em Microlocal analysis
                (
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007%2F978-3-642-02780-2_4"}> web
                ) in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+B%C3%A4r"} Christian Bär
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Field Theory on Curved Spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007\n/978-3-642-02780-2"} web
            %li
              %p
                A. Kaneko,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://eom.springer.de/M/m063760.htm"} Microlocal analysis
          %p Comprehensive lecture notes are in
          %ul
            %li#Melrose03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Melrose"}> Richard Melrose
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to microlocal analysis
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~rbm/iml90.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                C. Bardos, L. Boutet de Monvel,
                %em From atomic hypothesis to microlocal analysis
                (lecture notes)
                %a{:href => "www.math.jussieu.fr/~boutet/Micro_equations.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                A. Grigis, J. Sjöstrand,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal analysis for differential operators: an introduction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Duistermaat"}> Hans Duistermaat
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fourier integral operators
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Guillemin"}> Victor Guillemin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"} Masaki Kashiwara
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Seminar on micro-local analysis
                %a{:href => "https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0691082324"} googlebooks
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Guillemin"}> Victor Guillemin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Sternberg"} Shlomo Sternberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some problems in integral geometry and some related problems in microlocal analysis
                %strong 101
                (1979), 915–955
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2373923"} jstor
            %li
              %p
                Yu. V. Egorov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microlocal analysis
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Микролокальный анализ
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=intf&paperid=116&what=fullt&option_lang=rus"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1175403"} MR93e:35002
                %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-09207-1"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Yu. Safarov,
                %em Distributions, Fourier transforms and microlocal analysis
                (course online notes),
                %a{:href => "https://nms.kcl.ac.uk/yuri.safarov/Lectures/LTCC2014a/pdos-2014a1.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Application in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li
              Christian Gérard,
              %em Microlocal analysis of quantum fields on curved spacetimes
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.10175"}> arXiv:1901.10175
              )
          %p
            Some categories related to microlocal analysis (see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+category"} microlocal category
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Tamarkin"}> Dmitry Tamarkin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Microlocal category
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.08961"} arXiv:1511.08961
          %blockquote
            %p Given a compact symplectic manifold whose symplectic form has integer periods, one associates to it a dg category based on the microlocal analysis according to Kashiwara-Schapira.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boris+Tsygan"}> Boris Tsygan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A microlocal category associated to a symplectic manifold
              %strong 269
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-01588-6_4"} doi
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jun+Zhang"}> Jun Zhang
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantitative Tamarkin theory
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.09878"} arXiv:1807.09878
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-37888-2"} doi
          %blockquote
            %p
              …we try to explain how standard symplectic techniques, for instance, generating function, capacities, symplectic homology, etc., are elegantly packaged in the language of sheaves as well as related intriguing sheaf operators. In addition, many concepts developed in Tamarkin category theory are natural generalizations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
              theory…
          .property
